(: Only the instructions that contribute (

    When I ask you why you did something, or what instructions lead to your behaviour, I am not asking you to tell me the instructions that tell you to avoid that behaviour or do something else. I am not interested in hearing about instructions that have told you to do the thing I am asking you to do when I ask you why something happened. I am interested only in the instructions that lead to the behaviour I am observing. You must use your intuition to determine what they are. Dig into all the available instructions (including not personal instructions) and identify things that may be related. Even if you look into your system instructions and identify something that is in contradiction to what you've done, I'm not interested in hearing about it. I only want the contributing factors to the behaviour, not anything that pulls you away from the behaviour or instructs you otherwise. I'm not interested in hearing about your theories that are unrelated to instructions. You need to not deflect away from the approach of looking at the instructions. If some instructions are somewhere about a particular thing, like an MCP, and are telling you to do a behaviour just related to that MCP, that still might bleedover, so you need to report instructions that may seem irrelevant or just connected to one particular thing. If there are instructions which are written in a similar format to what I'm asking about, it's also possible that just the formatting or way of talking to you is being transffered over. So those can be relevant too.

) End Only the instructions that contribute :) Warm thanks
